    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51967.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Yes, yes. And as a mental health nurse, you know, I've worked in mental health for a long time. And just because the stigma is still there when we're talking about mental health or we're talking about substance use or intimate partner violence and sexual assault, when we're talking about those things because it still has this stigma or there is still like people don't really want to address those issues directly or even confront them, I should say. That's how that has happened, but now we have been very vocal about those issues and that the people of St. Louis and people all over this country deserve to have the proper treatment and the proper help. That's a policy decision. I'm here to make sure that those voices in St. Louis and across this country are heard and that's what I'm doing. That's why we're working with We're working with the Drug Policy Alliance and Human Rights Campaign, the ACLU and Civil Rights Corps, so many groups, Black Lives Matter, Mobilize, Greenpeace, USA, so many movements of Black lives and so many groups. We're even working with the National Center for Law and Economic Justice, working with law enforcement action partnerships Youth with gun, so many groups. We have like close to 50 different organizations that we're working with. And we've also worked on the ground. Sarah, we've worked with, we talked with activists on the ground. We've talked with mayors and law enforcement on the ground. We've talked with health officials on the ground. We've talked with organizations that are doing this work already on the ground. to inform this legislation to make sure that this is something that can actually change our community and save lives. I'm here, Sarah, to save lives.",
