    "turn_text": "is not when when i think about my patients who have who would live in uh... who have shown up uh... in in a clinic suffering with a mental health crisis they need help they need resources they need somebody to understand what they're going through they need that and they deserve that and so what we've seen when we know um... statistics uh... statistics to show up that uh... this is a problem that will only be solved on through legislation because when you have uh... these issues where people will not call the police, because they, you know, when someone is having a mental health crisis, when people with untreated mental health disorders are 16 times more likely to be killed during a police encounter than people without mental health disorders, when nearly one in four people killed by law enforcement officials have a mental health disorder, it makes people not want to call. That's not what we need. So that's why we are one thing that we have with this also, is this money that's for cities and states and localities, there is money there to be able to try 911 professionals to be able to reroute those calls to this division. There is money available for 988 for those mental health call centers. And the education says, hey, you know, have your people call this 988 number and we can dispatch a nurse or something like that. And let me say too, I've seen this work because there is organization in St. Louis that I worked very well with as a nurse, as a mental health nurse, and they would go out on these calls instead of police. And they saved lives. And I just want to see this on a bigger scale.",
